      "hints": [
        "Set $\\frac{1 - \\sin\\theta}{1 + \\sin\\theta} = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies 3\\sin\\theta = 1 \\implies \\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}$.",
        "Solve in $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ]$ to find $\\theta = 19.5^\\circ, 160.5^\\circ$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the proven identity from part (i) to simplify the equation:\n$$\\frac{1 - \\sin\\theta}{1 + \\sin\\theta} = \\frac{1}{2}$$",
        "Cross-multiply and solve for $\\sin\\theta$:\n$$2(1 - \\sin\\theta) = 1 + \\sin\\theta \\implies 2 - 2\\sin\\theta = 1 + \\sin\\theta \\implies 3\\sin\\theta = 1 \\implies \\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}$$",
        "Find the solutions for $\\theta$ in the range $0^\\circ \\le \\theta \\le 360^\\circ$:\n$$\\theta_1 = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\approx 19.47^\\circ \\approx 19.5^\\circ$$\n$$\\theta_2 = 180^\\circ - 19.47^\\circ \\approx 160.53^\\circ \\approx 160.5^\\circ$$\n$$(\\text{Solutions: } \\theta = 19.5^\\circ, \\quad 160.5^\\circ)$$"
      ],
